.TH LEGION-POWERCTL 8 "2026-08-07" "legion-powerctl 0.3.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
legion-powerctl \- configure Ryzen CPU power limits on Lenovo Legion laptops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B legion-powerctl
.RI COMMAND " [arguments]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B legion-powerctl
validates named profiles, applies RyzenAdj package-power and temperature limits,
and optionally controls powerprofilesctl, CPUFreq boundaries, boost, and AMD
P-State EPP. Its systemd service is a one-shot unit that runs once at boot.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B apply [PROFILE] [--dry-run] [--boot]
Apply a named profile or the selected active profile.
.TP
.B configure PROFILE [OPTIONS]
Create or update a profile. Options include --stapm, --slow, --fast, --temp,
--power-profile, --min-mhz, --max-mhz, --boost, --epp, --description,
--select, and --apply.
.TP
.B wizard [PROFILE]
Open an interactive profile editor.
.TP
.B select PROFILE [--apply]
Select the profile used at boot.
.TP
.B list
List profiles. The active profile is marked with an asterisk.
.TP
.B show [PROFILE]
Show a profile, defaulting to the active profile.
.TP
.B delete PROFILE [--force]
Delete a profile. The active profile is protected unless --force is used.
.TP
.B status [--json|--waybar]
Show configuration, service state, CPU driver, frequency range, and the most
recent successful apply record. With --json, print a single-line
machine-readable document carrying a schema_version field (currently 1) plus
every profile; the GUI and widgets consume this. With --waybar, print the JSON
object a Waybar custom module expects (text, alt, class, tooltip).
.TP
.B doctor
Run compatibility and installation checks, including RyzenAdj version,
Secure Boot/kernel-lockdown state, and conflicting power-management tools.
.TP
.B enable [--force]
Run doctor, then enable the boot service and apply the selected profile.
Refuses when doctor reports failures unless --force is supplied.
.TP
.B disable
Disable the boot service. Existing hardware state remains until firmware or a
reboot changes it.
.TP
.B restore-frequency [--boost on|off|unchanged]
Restore the hardware-reported CPUFreq minimum and maximum.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/legion-powerctl/config.conf
Selected active profile.
.TP
.I /etc/legion-powerctl/profiles.d/*.conf
Profile definitions.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/legion-powerctl.service
One-shot boot service.
.TP
.I /run/legion-powerctl/last-apply.env
Volatile record of the last successful apply operation.
.SH GRAPHICAL INTERFACE
.B legion-powerctl-gui
opens an unprivileged Qt control panel (requires the optional pyside6
package). It reads state through 'status --json' and performs privileged
actions through pkexec. The shipped policy defines one action per subcommand
(io.github.alexmacra.legion-powerctl.configure, .apply, .select, .delete,
.enable, .disable), each pinned with exec.argv1. Editing, applying, and
selecting profiles use auth_admin_keep, which polkit retains for a few
minutes; treat those three as one trust tier, because
.B configure
accepts --select and --apply and so reaches what the other two do. Deleting a
profile and enabling or disabling the boot service always prompt, so a
retained profile-editing authorization cannot be reused to remove a profile
or to install boot persistence.
.SH NOTES
This project is not affiliated with Lenovo, AMD, or CachyOS. Legion is a
trademark of Lenovo. Fire Range and other HX-series CPUs require RyzenAdj
0.19.0 or newer.
.PP
RyzenAdj state is volatile. Boot persistence means reapplying the selected
profile once during startup; it does not mean writing permanent firmware
settings. Firmware profile changes and suspend/resume may overwrite limits on
some laptops.
.PP
On newer Ryzen families, RyzenAdj may successfully apply settings even when
its information or power-metric table is unavailable.
.SH SAFETY
Profile validation prevents malformed input but does not establish that a
specific wattage or temperature is appropriate for every CPU or laptop.
Monitor temperatures and test workloads incrementally.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR powerprofilesctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
